The moving average system is \[y(t)=\frac{1}{T}\int_{t-T}^{t}u(\tau)\,d\tau.\] This is an LTI system with impulse response \[h(t)=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac{1}{T}, &amp; 0\le t\le T,\\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}\] Its frequency response is \[H(j\omega)=\frac{1}{T}\int_{0}^{T}e^{-j\omega t}\,dt
=e^{-j\omega T/2}\frac{\sin(\omega T/2)}{\omega T/2}.\] The input frequency is \[f=\frac{1}{2T}\;\text{Hz},\] so \[\omega=2\pi f=\frac{\pi}{T}.\] Hence, \[\angle H(j\omega)
=-\frac{\omega T}{2}
=-\frac{\pi}{2}
=-90^\circ.\] Since \[\frac{\sin(\pi/2)}{\pi/2}&gt;0,\] there is no additional phase shift. Therefore, the output lags the input by \[\boxed{90}\]

The given impulse response is \[g(t)=
\begin{cases}
1, &amp; 0\le t\le 1,\\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}\] For two cascaded LTI systems, the overall impulse response is the convolution \[h(t)=g(t)*g(t).\] Evaluating the convolution, \[h(t)=
\begin{cases}
t, &amp; 0\le t\le 1,\\
2-t, &amp; 1\le t\le 2,\\
0, &amp; \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}\] This is a triangular waveform. Its maximum value occurs at \[t=1,\] giving \[h_{\max}=1.\] \[\boxed{\text{(d) }1}\]

Given \[\mathcal{L}\left\{2\sqrt{\frac{t}{\pi}}\right\}=s^{-3/2}.\] Differentiate both sides with respect to \(s\): \[\mathcal{L}\{tf(t)\}
=-\frac{dF(s)}{ds}.\] Here, \[f(t)=2\sqrt{\frac{t}{\pi}}, \qquad
F(s)=s^{-3/2}.\] Hence, \[-\frac{d}{ds}\left(s^{-3/2}\right)
=\frac{3}{2}s^{-5/2}.\] Also, \[tf(t)=2t\sqrt{\frac{t}{\pi}}
=\frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}}t^{3/2}.\] Therefore, \[\mathcal{L}\left\{\frac{2}{\sqrt{\pi}}t^{3/2}\right\}
=\frac{3}{2}s^{-5/2}.\] Since \[g(t)=\sqrt{\frac{1}{\pi t}}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\pi}}t^{-1/2},\] we have \[2t\,g(t)=2\sqrt{\frac{t}{\pi}}.\] Applying the time-multiplication property, \[\mathcal{L}\{2t\,g(t)\}
=-\frac{dG(s)}{ds}
=s^{-3/2},\] where \(G(s)=\mathcal{L}\{g(t)\}\). Thus, \[\frac{dG(s)}{ds}=-s^{-3/2}.\] Integrating, \[G(s)=2s^{-1/2}.\] Hence, \[\mathcal{L}\left\{\sqrt{\frac{1}{\pi t}}\right\}
=s^{-1/2}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(b) }s^{-1/2}}\]

